    "faq": {
      "kicker": "Common questions",
      "h2": "Things people ask before they come.",
      "faq": {
        "items": [
          {
            "summary": "Do I need an appointment?",
            "p": "It helps. Send a WhatsApp message with your name and the kind of matter, and we give you a time slot so you are not waiting. Walk-ins between 5 pm and 9 pm are seen if the advocate is free."
          },
          {
            "summary": "Can you tell me on the phone whether I have a case?",
            "p": "Not properly. Most matters depend on the papers. Bring them to the chamber and you will get a straight answer in thirty minutes. If there is no case worth filing, we say so and you pay only the consultation fee."
          },
          {
            "summary": "How long does a mutual consent divorce take?",
            "p": "Usually six to eight months from the first motion to the decree, with the six-month waiting period in between. The court can shorten the wait in some situations. We explain this at the first meeting."
          },
          {
            "summary": "Will I have to come to court on every date?",
            "p": "No. For most dates the advocate appears alone. You need to be present for evidence, for certain statements, and on the day of a final order. We tell you a week in advance."
          },
          {
            "summary": "Do you take cases outside Kolkata?",
            "p": "Matters at Alipore, Sealdah, Barasat and Howrah courts, yes. Other districts only when there is a strong reason, because travel adds to your cost."
          },
          {
            "summary": "Can I pay in parts?",
            "p": "Yes. Court matters are usually paid in two or three parts: at filing, at evidence, and at argument. The split is written into the fee quote. UPI, bank transfer and cash are all accepted, with a receipt."
          }
        ]
      }
